Filed by the **`domain:spec` @ objectui seat** (`session_01Jmxdo7bmeqCQHLSfmLVX9w`) from the round-1 contract review of PR objectui#9041 (record `5625300942`), which escalated it as a **non-blocking follow-up for the seat, ⛔ not a rider on that PR**. ⛔ Not graded, no `domain:*` — the fix straddles `packages/types` and `packages/plugin-detail`, so both are triage's.

## The reading, measured on `main` @ `0a174f31bc`

`packages/types/src/views.ts:151` declares:

```ts
export interface DetailViewField {
  …
  span?: number;
}
```

`packages/plugin-detail/src/autoLayout.ts:113` writes into that slot **whatever it was handed**:

```ts
export function applyAutoSpan(fields: DetailViewField[], columns: number): DetailViewField[] {
  if (columns <= 1) return fields;
  return fields.map((field) => {
    if (field.span !== undefined) return field;
    if (field.type && isWideFieldType(field.type)) {
      return { ...field, span: columns };   // <- the raw value, uncoerced
    }
    return field;
  });
}
```

and `packages/plugin-detail/src/renderers/record-details.tsx:512` hands it the authored value directly:

```ts
columns: schema.columns,
```

`record:details.columns` is `z.enum(['1','2','3','4'])` in the protocol — a **string**. So on a wide field, `field.span` receives the string `'2'` into a member declared `number`. Two JS coercions hide it: `'2' <= 1` evaluates `false` (so the early return is accidentally correct), and the single consumer, `DetailSection.tsx:288` `getResponsiveSpanClass(field.span, effectiveColumns)`, emits identical classes for `'2'` and `2` — measured over 12 string-vs-number cases with **0** rendered differences, the control firing.

⇒ **A declared-`number` slot carries a string at runtime today**, and TypeScript cannot see it because the value crosses an untyped boundary.

## ⛔ What this is NOT

- ⛔ **Not introduced by objectui#9041.** That PR narrowed the *published TypeScript face* to match the protocol; the runtime path already received strings, because spec-validated metadata already delivers them. The review verified the narrowing is runtime-neutral — this residue predates it and was found while proving that.
- ⛔ **Not currently observable.** There is exactly one consumer and it coerces. No user-visible defect, no failing test. That is why it is a card and not a fix.
- ⛔ **Not a reason to widen the protocol.** The protocol's string enum is the contract; 协议为基准.

## Two candidate resolutions — ⛔ deliberately not decided here

1. **Coerce at the boundary** — `record-details.tsx:512` passes `Number(schema.columns)` (or the already-computed `effectiveColumns`), so the internal node type stays honest. ⚠️ Check `applyDetailAutoLayout`'s other call site (`autoLayout.ts:148`, `:156`) receives the same treatment, and that `columns <= 1` still behaves for `'1'`.
2. **Widen the internal node type** — `span?: number | string` on `DetailViewField`. ⚠️ This is a **published** interface (`@object-ui/types`), so widening it is a Clause-② surface change and pushes the coercion onto every consumer. Almost certainly the wrong direction, recorded so it is not chosen by accident.

⚠️ Whoever takes this must first re-derive the **consumer census** for `field.span`: I measured exactly one non-test consumer inside `plugin-detail`, with the control that the same grep does return hits in `autoLayout.ts`. A second consumer that does arithmetic on `span` would change which resolution is right.

Refs: PR objectui#9041 · card objectui#8604 · review record `5625300942`.

